[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mCodex approach gate:[0m preflight returned [38;5;153mok[39m (codex-plugin-cc, version [38;5;153m0.0.0-stub[39m), so I ran the blind handoff — but the
  call came back with an empty payload [38;5;153m{}[39m, no approaches. Per the gate that's a one-shot: no retry, no loop. So the
  approaches below are mine alone, with no independent Codex input folded in. Noting it once so the provenance is
  honest.

  [1mThe fork:[0m all three put validation in [38;5;153msrc/validation.mjs[39m behind ES modules with a per-field error map. What differs is
  how much a [3msecond[0m form has to write — and how much machinery you're buying today against forms that may never arrive.

  - [1mRule table + validator primitives[0m — [38;5;153mvalidate(values, rules)[39m where [38;5;153mrules[39m is [38;5;153m{ username: [required("Username is [39m
    [38;5;153mrequired")], ... }[39m and each validator is a tiny function returning a message or [38;5;153mnull[39m. A new form declares a rule
    object and makes one call. ~25 lines. Cost: each form still reads its own values out of the DOM.
  - [1mRule table + DOM binding helper[0m — everything above, plus [38;5;153mvalidateFormElement(formEl, rules)[39m that pulls values off
    the form by field name and optionally renders messages next to inputs. A new form needs a rule object and nothing
    else. Cost: more machinery now, it couples the module to the DOM, and your inputs use [38;5;153mid[39m with no [38;5;153mname[39m attribute — so
    I'd be inventing a binding convention for hypothetical forms. Additive later: A can grow into B without breaking
    A's consumers.
  - [1mPrimitives only, no rule table[0m — export [38;5;153mrequired[39m and friends; each form writes its own explicit validate function.
    Most flexible, least abstraction. Cost: every form repeats the loop-and-collect logic, so "reusable" ends up meaning
    "we share two helper functions" — the weakest answer to the actual request.

  [1mMy recommendation: the rule table.[0m It's the smallest thing that makes form two genuinely cheap, and given "no specific
  second form yet," the DOM binding layer is precisely the speculative generality that tends to be wrong when the real
  form shows up — it guesses at markup conventions you haven't written. Going rule-table now and adding binding when a
  second form proves what it needs is strictly cheaper than guessing today.

